---
import { business } from "../data/business";

// The 10 year workmanship guarantee is the figure to confirm with the client.
// The materials slot deliberately carries no number: manufacturer cover varies
// by product, so a single headline figure would be wrong on most jobs. Do not
// add one without a specific manufacturer to attach it to.
const years = business.workmanshipGuaranteeYears;
---
<section class="bg-slate text-white">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold rule">What our guarantee actually is</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-steel-light">
        Every roofing van in south London says "all work guaranteed" on the side, and it costs
        nothing to paint. The question worth asking is whether anything gets written down. Work
        carried out by {business.name} comes with a {years} year workmanship guarantee as a real
        document - dated, signed, specific to the job we did on your house - handed to you at
        completion along with the photographs. The materials carry their manufacturers' own
        cover on top of that, and because it varies from product to product your paperwork names
        the one that applies to yours rather than quoting an averaged figure at you.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-steel-light">
        It is worth understanding why that changes how we work. A guarantee lasting a decade is
        only affordable to us if the repairs do not come back, so failed material comes off
        rather than being sealed over, junctions get lead rather than a fillet of cement, and on
        the exposed ground towards Biggin Hill and Coney Hall we fix mechanically instead of
        trusting mortar. Anything we find that we cannot deal with the same day gets
        photographed and said out loud, because a problem kept quiet now becomes our problem
        within {years} years.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-steel-light">
        In plain terms: if water finds its way back through something we repaired, we come back
        and put it right at no cost to you. It is worth asking anyone else quoting for the job
        to put the same thing in writing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-brand/40 bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-light leading-tight">{years} yrs</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white uppercase tracking-wide">Workmanship, in writing</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-brand/40 bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-light leading-tight">£0</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white uppercase tracking-wide">To call us back under it</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-steel bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-white leading-tight">Insured</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-steel-light uppercase tracking-wide">Public liability cover in place</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-steel bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-white leading-tight">Named</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-steel-light uppercase tracking-wide">Manufacturer cover, per job</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
